I’ll inspect the measurement, chemical context, and the supplied framework notes first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine the multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the final artifacts tied to the saved `final.gpx` and verify that the exported profile and fractions reproduce that project.

The chemistry points first to MgWO₄, but the raw scan has a high, strongly sloping background and could contain residual MgO/WO₃ or an intermediate. I’m now separating the peak inventory from that background and querying the permitted crystallographic library for Mg–W–O candidates before committing to a phase count.

Peak positions establish three chemically coherent fingerprints: wolframite-type MgWO₄ dominates; monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ accounts for the 23–35° triplets/splittings; and MgO is supported independently at about 43.21°, 62.54°, and 75.00°. I’m carrying all three into quantitative refinement and will test whether the MgO scale remains significant once overlap and background are modeled.

The three-phase scales are all positive and well determined in the first Rietveld pass (roughly 77.6% MgWO₄, 14.1% WO₃, 8.2% MgO), so MgO is not an overlap artifact. The remaining mismatch is dominated by a low-angle intensity deficit and phase-dependent peak widths—exactly the specimen roughness and crystallite-size/microstrain effects indicated by this measurement geometry—so I’m refining those before touching atomic coordinates.

A Le Bail check confirms that the remaining residual is profile-shape limited rather than an omitted phase: no new unmatched peak family appears when intensities are freed. The isotropic broadening model drives common `U` to its physical ceiling, so I’m testing the three symmetry-allowed uniaxial microstrain directions for the dominant MgWO₄ phase; this should separate real anisotropy from common instrumental width without letting parameters go negative.

The best profile model selects MgWO₄ microstrain along [100], while MgO and WO₃ remain adequately isotropic. Because profile terms are partially correlated without an external standard, I’m now holding that physically bounded profile and refining the quantities the pattern constrains more directly: common per-phase displacement parameters, W-site coordinates first, then oxygen coordinates. This also tests whether the 1960 WO₃ reference is structurally adequate rather than merely peak-position compatible.

The heavy-atom refinements stay chemically modest, but the WO₃ reflection-intensity residuals remain strongly hkl-specific (for example, 020 is underpredicted while 022 is overpredicted). That is evidence to test the alternative room-temperature monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ references in the frozen COD, rather than forcing oxygen coordinates to unphysical shifts. I’m comparing those parents under the same fixed profile model now.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to three crystalline phases—wolframite MgWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and periclase MgO. I’m now finishing the constrained structure/profile refinement, with special care not to let the oxygen coordinates or peak-broadening terms drift into chemically implausible values.

The current limiting mismatch is angle-dependent intensity, not unindexed peaks. I’m testing a physically constrained Bragg–Brentano surface-roughness correction; this chiefly determines whether the phase fractions move by a few percent, while the three-phase identification itself remains stable.

The data strongly support two real specimen/instrument effects: a small MgWO₄ [001] texture term and low effective polarization. Both improve the fit without changing phase identity; the phase fractions are currently near 75.2% MgWO₄, 15.5% WO₃, and 9.4% MgO. I’m now separating angular calibration from sample displacement so the refined unit cells are not forced to absorb peak-position errors.

The constant zero-offset and specimen-height terms are almost perfectly correlated, so I’m treating that decomposition as a calibration uncertainty instead of accepting an unstable simultaneous refinement. A bounded grid is being used to select the geometry that best preserves the full-range peak positions; this is the main uncertainty on the absolute unit-cell values, not on phase identification.

The physically bounded replay is now around 8% Rwp; the earlier lower number depended on a polarization value outside the stated instrument range, so I discarded it. I’m refining instrument and phase broadening in separate stages now, keeping all size and strain terms inside the known laboratory ranges.

The phase-temperature factors can be constrained from the angular intensity falloff for MgWO₄ and WO₃, but MgO has only six reflections and its temperature factor is strongly coupled to its scale. The best constrained model is converging near 74.7 wt% MgWO₄, 15.6 wt% WO₃, and 9.7 wt% MgO; I’ll report an additional systematic uncertainty for that MgO fraction rather than treating the small least-squares error as complete.

The structural choice is now stable: the monoclinic WO₃ parent fits materially better than the common alternative setting/orthorhombic model, while an unconstrained triclinic model only gains a negligible amount by adding cell degrees of freedom. I’ve retained the chemically justified monoclinic structure and am writing the final replayable project and exports from a last constrained refinement.

The final constrained refinement converged cleanly with no residual parameter shifts: Rwp = 7.86%, Rp = 5.48%, and GOF = 2.75. The normalized result is 74.92 wt% MgWO₄, 15.46 wt% monoclinic WO₃, and 9.62 wt% MgO; I’m validating project/CIF/profile consistency and chemical geometry before writing the brief uncertainty report.

